{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791594519,"nonce":"vAEErGK/vf8BOfdCmDLhNL1zF6s=","title":"Interaction states the host never sets","message":"The engine parses sixteen state pseudo-classes (css_states) but the host sets seven: hover, active, focus, focus-visible, focus-within, disabled and enabled (Host#bits in src/native/host.ts, checked). checked, indeterminate, placeholder-shown, valid, invalid, user-valid, user-invalid, required and optional never match, so a sheet that writes input:checked styles nothing.\n\nUntil there are built-in inputs (see the element behaviours issue), a component that wants to style its state mirrors it into an attribute and the sheet uses [data-checked]; the Base UI adapter does.\n\nScope: let a node carry these states, set from the host (an API such as element.setState('checked', true)) and, once inputs exist, from the input layer. TypeScript only.\n\nAcceptance: a rule on :checked restyles in place when the state is set and cleared, with no change to the node's attributes.","files":null}]}